{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Not because you are numerous.</b> The sense of the verse is that it would have been fitting for the king's glory to be in the multitude, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 14:28): ״ברב עם הדרת מלך״ — 'in the multitude of people is the king's glory'; and yet He desired you and chose you, as He mentioned above: ״בך בחר ה׳ אלהיך להיות לו לעם סגולה״ — 'HaShem your Elohim has chosen you to be His treasured people.' There is no officer, magistrate, or ruler among the angels on high, but you are the סגולה (treasured possession) of the Holy One, blessed be He, under His hand; and therefore you will not stray to worship idolatry, the gods of the peoples. Thus wrote the Rambam (the Ramban), of blessed memory." "<b>And it appears</b> proper to interpret 'not because you are numerous' — not by reason of your multitude did HaShem desire you in Egypt to bring you out from there and choose you to be His people. And Scripture alludes in this that Israel are more numerous than all the peoples, and yet He says: the desire and the choice are not for your multitude, for ״כי אתם המעט״ — 'because you are the fewest.' That is to say: even if you who are the many were fewer than all the peoples, He would still desire and choose you. And He gives the reason why, and says: ״מאהבת ה׳ אתכם ומשמרו את השבועה״ — 'because of HaShem's love for you and His keeping the oath.' And we would need to force the interpretation and say thus, because now, in the fortieth year, Moses testified and said (Deuteronomy 1:10): ״והנכם היום ככוכבי השמים לרוב״ — 'and you are today as the stars of heaven for multitude' — if so, how can he say 'because you are the fewest of all the peoples,' when behold they are numerous as the stars of heaven for multitude?" "<b>And it is possible</b> to say further, that the weak (rafeh) ״מ״ם״ testifies to this: for if it had said המעט with a dagesh, I would understand that they are fewer than all the nations; but it comes with a rafeh, as in (Numbers 16:9): ״המעט מכם כי הבדיל״ — 'is it a small thing for you that He separated'; (ibid.): ״המעט כי העליתנו״ — 'is it a small thing that you brought us up' — and the meaning will be ״כי אתם״ in the language of greatness and pre-eminence: He says, 'you who are chosen — המעט from all the peoples are you' — not you, but rather you are more than all, and yet not because of your multitude, but only by reason of the love and the oath. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״כי אתם המעט״ — HaShem is few — the families of Israel were seventy, deficient compared to the families of Noah's sons, which were seventy, while Israel were sixty-five families, as they were counted in the section of Pinchas. And the word חשק is the language of binding, from the language of (Exodus 27:10): ״וחשוקיהם כסף״ — 'and their hooks silver' — for they are fixed and fastened tightly; and the meaning is that He bound Himself to you with a strong bond that will never be separated from you forever." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> 'Not because you are numerous' — not because you exalt yourselves, but because you are humble and humble yourselves before Me, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 136:23): ״שבשפלנו זכר לנו כי לעולם חסדו״ — 'when we were lowly He remembered us, for His lovingkindness is forever.' This is ״כי אתם המעט מכל העמים״ — 'because you are the fewest of all the peoples.' Said the Holy One, blessed be He: At the time when I bestow greatness upon you, you diminish yourselves. I gave greatness to Abraham — he said (Genesis 18:27): ״ואנכי עפר ואפר״ — 'and I am dust and ashes.' I gave greatness to Moses and Aaron — they said (Exodus 16:22–23): ״ונחנו מה״ — 'and what are we.' I gave greatness to David — he said (Psalms 22:7): ״ואנכי תולעת ולא איש״ — 'and I am a worm, and no man.' But the nations of the world are not so: I gave greatness to Nimrod — he said (Genesis 11:4): ״הבה נבנה לנו עיר״ — 'come, let us build ourselves a city.' I gave greatness to Pharaoh — he said (Exodus 5:2): ״מי ה׳ אשר אשמע בקולו״ — 'Who is HaShem that I should obey His voice.' To Sennacherib — he said (Isaiah 36:20): ״מי בכל אלהי הארצות האלה אשר הצילו את ארצם מידי כי יציל ה׳ את ירושלים מידי״ — 'Who among all the gods of these lands that delivered their land from my hand, that HaShem should deliver Jerusalem from my hand?' To Nebuchadnezzar — he said (Isaiah 14): ״אעלה על במתי עב״ וגו׳ — 'I will ascend the heights of the cloud,' etc. To Hiram king of Tyre — he said (Ezekiel 28:2): ״אל אני מושב אלהים ישבתי בלב ימים״ — 'I am El; I sit in the seat of Elohim, in the heart of the seas.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The text reads הרמב״ם (הרמב״ן) ז״ל — both names appear, suggesting a variant or correction; translated as written.", 2 "The Midrashic reading 'HaShem is few (ה׳ מעט)' is a traditional play reading the divine name in place of the standard pause; translated hyper-literally as it appears. The argument comparing seventy to sixty-five families is translated exactly as written, including the apparent repetition of 'seventy.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:36.645010424Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}